{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1555086-new-shapeshifter-dragons-of-the-land-the-mo-o","id":1555086,"title":"[New Shapeshifter] Dragons of The Land: The Mo'o","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-11-04T19:21:44-08:00","id":1555086,"title":"[New Shapeshifter] Dragons of The Land: The Mo'o","content":"Legends have long described the islands that make up Hawaii as possessing a tremendous connection to the realms of gods and spiritual beings of all stripes, the Rainbow State being a sort of crossroads between the mundane and the divine. Like most stories in the Chronicles of Darkness there is a sizable kernel of truth to the legends, this beloved tourist spot is redolent with spiritual activity: Hawai'i itself being home to one of the largest and most powerful Loci on the entire planet, Mount K\u012blauea, along with many smaller loci across the various islands. This veritable flood of essence supports and emboldens a wide variety of spirit courts and their esoteric politicking across Hawaii's Hisil reflection and the material world alike, aided by the unusually thin Gauntlet, to the point where many locals on some level are aware of the spirits and their antics- some even downplaying the supernatural as little more than &quot;one of those things&quot; that makes their home special, while others recognize the danger and do their best to safeguard themselves and their loved ones through rite and tradition to appease the <b>akua</b> and their servants.<br /> <br /> Despite the vibrant and concerningly intrusive nature of the Shadow in this corner of the world there is an astonishingly small population of Uratha who call Hawaii home, with only a handful of packs scattered about the islands save for the scions of one tribe. Even stranger, the spirit courts of Hawaii do something that has long baffled Bone Shadow shamans: namely they police themselves. Logic would dictate that with a Hisil ecosystem as powerful and pervasive as the one in Hawaii, and one with so few werewolves to manage it, there would be chaos as spirits war with each other for dominance on both sides of the Gauntlet unfettered and constrained only by their ambitions... yet here in the Rainbow State it doesn't happen. Yes, occasionally the Uratha step in when one of the upstart akua get too big for their britches and cause trouble in territories they have no business being, but there are rules in play: the children of Wolf keep to their borders, save for the freedom to punish the odd troublemaker that's foolish enough to try to muscle in on the werewolf's turf, and the spirit courts keep to theirs and are allowed to keep themselves in check by their own methods. Another rule, a harsher one, is that those whose Uratha bound to Winter Wolf are forbidden from setting foot or paw on the sands of Hawaii. This law has existed, and tested the mettle of werewolves, for centuries and for good reason.<br /> <br /> The reason is the very method of self-policing that the akua of Hawaii implement: a population of spirit-claimed known as the <b>Kupua</b>. The Kupua are ubiquitous in the politics of the akua: they are intermediaries between spirit broods, proxy soldiers during violent disputes over territories on both sides of the Gauntlet, even liaisons and emissaries with humans. Their most important function, however, is safeguarding the akua and the human natives of Hawaii and preventing any of the courts from overstepping their bounds and running wild. The Kupua serve the spirit courts but are not loyal to any court exclusively- they are the willing union of local islanders and akua, bound together by a sacred pact: demigod mediators and peacekeepers who have kept order for as long as anyone can remember. The existence of these Claimed has been the source of deep controversy for the People, even the instigating factor of more than one war between the native spirits and the Uratha, but for the past few decades a tenuous understanding has been reached: a sort of Cold War style non-aggression agreement between the courts, the Kupua, and the Forsaken. Abide by the rules, and everyone is happy, or is close to happy as can be approximated in the spirit's case. Don't, and sometimes the Cold War grows Hot for a few nights before settling back down to an uneasy &quot;peace&quot;... but sometimes these hot flashes are more of a benefit to the status quo than a detriment- if a particularly annoying rival or an underperforming Kapua ends up being lunch/impromptu stress relief for a testy pack of werewolves... well that's one less idiot and liability scratched off the list, it's a win-win really!<br /> <br /> For a long time, this balancing act has maintained a happy equilibrium, something nearly unheard of in most parts of the world and has attributed to a Shadow ecosystem that's been able to thrive in ways most werewolves would kill for... but something has caused this power balance to shift in recent years. Loci have begun changing, their resonances inexplicably shifting from what they were and generating essence incompatible with the spirits that sup from them. There are whispers amongst akua of beings slinking along the periphery of their courts borders that are neither spirit, Kupua, or Uratha.. cold blooded beasts that wear faces of men and great lizards. Creatures that can take on the guise of innocuous fixtures in the scenery: trees, hills, bridges.. lying in wait until a victim gets juuuuust close enough before swallowing them whole. Stories of vicious dragons clad in scales that burn with the fury of a wildfire, or armored in unbreakable stone, or even stranger things such being cloaked in clinical depression or covered snout to tail with the faces of a thousand yowling cats. No one truly knows what these strange monsters are, but a name has begun being whispered on the wind, a warning to all who have become complacent with the way things currently are:<br /> <br /> <i>Mirskmatha.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Mo'o.</b><br /> <br /> The dragons are here, and this land will be theirs.<br /> _____<br /> <br /> Hey all, Korogra here! After a long, long time I've been bitten by the creativity bug and have decided to present to you an idea for a shapeshifter that's floated around in my brain on and off again for years: over a decade ago I ran a werewolf 1e campaign in Hawaii and had made allusions to a race of reptilian shapeshifters that once called the islands home but were seemingly extinct.. and recently I've been struck with inspiration to fully stat them out for Werewolf second edition. But as I began brainstorming, it evolved into creating a reimagined chronicle setting for Hawaii and what the spiritual ecosystem might look like, and how these lizard shifters disrupt it. So, this post is a sort of preface to give brief context of how the Shadow of Hawaii operates and what the status quo has been like, leading up to the emergence of the Mo'o... and a bit of a hint to their origins.<br /> <br /> My next update post will cover the Mirskmatha in full and should be up sometime later this week!<br /> <br /> Thank you for reading, and I look forward to see what you think of them once I have them up. As always, I hope you all have a wonderful day!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1686,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","user":"Korogra","date":". 11-04-2025, 10:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:45:19-08:00","id":1555102,"title":"","content":"I was actually reading up on Mo'o yesterday, so this is extra entertaining to me. Mo'o are an iconic creature of Hawaiian folklore, even though lizards - their mundane inspiration - seem to be recent arrivals to the islands. You seem to be leaning into that contradiction, with your Mo'o arriving (or resurfacing) recently.<br /> <br /> I see how you took their ability to imitate features of the environment. You must... Must! ...use the fact that deceased Mo'o become permanent parts of the land.<br /> <br /> I can't recall any sources where Mo'o wear skins, armors and concepts with equal ease. Did you find something I didn't, or is this your creative decision?<br /> <br /> The setting in general is also a nice surprise. It's cool to see assumptions of the game bent to their breaking point, while still staying within the game's themes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T05:32:36-08:00","id":1555112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Teatime</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1555086-new-shapeshifter-dragons-of-the-land-the-mo-o?p=1555102#post1555102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was actually reading up on Mo'o yesterday, so this is extra entertaining to me. Mo'o are an iconic creature of Hawaiian folklore, even though lizards - their mundane inspiration - seem to be recent arrivals to the islands. You seem to be leaning into that contradiction, with your Mo'o arriving (or resurfacing) recently.<br /> <br /> I see how you took their ability to imitate features of the environment. You must... Must! ...use the fact that deceased Mo'o become permanent parts of the land.<br /> <br /> I can't recall any sources where Mo'o wear skins, armors and concepts with equal ease. Did you find something I didn't, or is this your creative decision?<br /> <br /> The setting in general is also a nice surprise. It's cool to see assumptions of the game bent to their breaking point, while still staying within the game's themes.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I\u2019ll go into it in detail with their write up, that\u2019s indeed a direction I\u2019m going for! The Mirskmatha are indeed something new, though there are actual Mo\u2019o spirits, while rare, that exist as well.<br /> <br /> I think you\u2019ll be pleased to see what I have planned for what happens when Mirskmatha die <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.<br /> <br /> And yes, this essence armor is creative liberty on my part.<br /> <br /> Thank you, I\u2019m having a lot of fun with this reimagined setting!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}